forti-cli (0.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Home, certificates and profiles
    - One forti home: `$FORTI_HOME`, else `~/.forti` (0700), for config.json,
      credentials/, state/, cache/ and certs/. `FORTI_CONFIG_DIR`,
      `FORTI_STATE_DIR`, `FORTI_CACHE_DIR` still override. A pre-0.2 XDG setup
      (`~/.config/forti`, `~/.local/state/forti`, `~/.cache/forti`) stays in
      use until migrated.
    - New `forti home` (every location, legacy flag) and `forti home migrate`
      (renames config.json, credentials/, state and cache into the home; modes
      kept; config.json last; idempotent; never overwrites a home config.json;
      refuses while a `FORTI_*_DIR` override is set).
    - `forti doctor` has a first check, `home`, that warns on a pre-0.2 setup.
    - New `forti cert create [--profile] [--days N] [--force] [--keychain]`: a
      private CA and a client certificate (CN `forti-<profile>`, RSA 3072,
      SHA-256) in `<home>/certs/<profile>/` through the `openssl` command
      (argv, no shell). The PKCS#12 uses PBE-SHA1-3DES with a SHA-1 MAC
      (OpenSSL 3 without the legacy provider, LibreSSL and FortiOS read it).
      Sets the profile's client_cert and certificate password, prints the
      password-reading command (never the password) and the FortiOS steps
      (`config vpn certificate ca`, `config user peer`, `config user peergrp`,
      `config system api-user` `peer-auth`/`peer-group`). Plus
      `forti cert show` and `forti cert remove`.
    - New SecretRef source `file` (absolute path; owner-only regular file, as
      the file store requires); `forti configure --cert-password-file PATH`.
    - New `forti profile set NAME key=value...` (validated as config.json is;
      re-binds stored secrets when host or port change),
      `forti profile rename OLD NEW` (moves stored secrets, `certs/OLD`,
      references and the default) and `forti profile copy SOURCE NEW` (settings
      only, no secrets).
    - `forti configure` on an existing profile prefills the API key command and
      keeps the current certificate password source on Enter.
    - Debian: `forti-cli` depends on `openssl`.
    - `forti install mcp --scope workspace`: register forti-mcp as project
      scope in the forti home (~/.forti or $FORTI_HOME), the recommended
      default and first interactive choice
      + scaffold, created and updated idempotently, never overwriting an edited
        file: `.mcp.json`, `.codex/config.toml`, the skill in `.claude/skills`
        and `.agents/skills`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` (`@AGENTS.md`), and
        `.forti-workspace.json` recording what forti wrote
      + secret hygiene: `.claude/settings.json` gains `permissions.deny` rules
        (`Read(/credentials/**)`, `Read(/certs/**/*.pass)`,
        `Read(/certs/**/*.p12)`, `Read(/certs/**/ca/**)`,
        `Edit(/config.json)`), merged into an existing file; Codex gets
        `default_permissions = "forti-workspace"`, a permission profile that
        denies the same paths and makes config.json read-only for sandboxed
        commands
      + Codex trust for the forti home only with consent (asked interactively,
        default no; or `--trust-codex-workspace`): exactly
        `[projects."<home>"] trust_level = "trusted"` is added to the global
        config with a format-preserving edit, and removed on uninstall only if
        forti added it
    - Home directory guard: project or local scope with $HOME as the project
      warns and asks "Are you sure?" (default no); without a terminal it is
      refused unless `--force`
    - `forti mcp status` shows the workspace scope and scaffold health (JSON:
      `workspace`); `forti mcp uninstall --scope workspace` removes the
      registration and forti's scaffold files, never config.json, credentials,
      certs or state
    - Skill: mentions the workspace convention and that the forti home's
      secrets are off limits

 -- Cooper Larson <cooper@valkyrianlabs.com>  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:02:53 +0000

forti-cli (0.1.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Documentation: the docs package (docs set forti-cli) is rewritten in
    Payload Markdown directives (TOCs, tabs, steps, callouts, cards, badges,
    buttons), with new summary tables for commands, exit statuses, plan states
    and rollback operations. The forti binary and the package contents are
    unchanged.

